  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1010 AM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nWARM AND DRY THROUGH THE WEEKEND: \n\nHigh pressure centered over the Southwest will keep warm and dry\nconditions in place through the weekend and beyond. Some\nguidance suggests afternoon convection is possible over the \nsouthern Colorado mountains, mostly on Sunday, as some moisture \ncirculates through our area with the high pressure. Forcing \nwill be very limited while high pressure is in place, but \ndaytime heating may be enough to get a few isolated showers and \nthunderstorms going over the southern high terrain Sunday \nafternoon. Otherwise, dry weather is expected. High temperatures\nwill be 5 to 15 degrees above normal this weekend, with the \nmost anomalous temperatures occurring north of I-70. \n\nWORKWEEK OUTLOOK:\n\nThe forecast for Monday looks similar to Sunday's forecast, with \ntemperatures 5 to 15 degrees above normal and afternoon \nconvection over the southern high terrain while moisture \ncontinues to circulate within the center of high pressure. Some \ndeterministic guidance continues to show the high pressure \nbreaking down Monday night into Tuesday, and a shortwave trough\nmoving across our area on Tuesday. This shortwave could support\nshowers and thunderstorms across more of our higher terrain \nareas, but both forcing and moisture do not look substantial \nenough to support significant rainfall. After Tuesday, warm and \ndry conditions with isolated afternoon thunderstorms over the  \nhigh terrain is the daily forecast through the workweek.",
